coder › Implemented `toRoman` and `fromRoman` in `src/roman.ts` and added `src/roman.test.ts`.

What changed
- `src/roman.ts`
  - `toRoman(n)` builds the numeral greedily from a descending value table (`M`, `CM`, `D`, `CD`, `C`, `XC`, `L`, `XL`, `X`, `IX`, `V`, `IV`, `I`). It rejects anything that isn't an integer in `1..3999` with a `RangeError` (`Number.isInteger` catches `NaN`, `Infinity`, and non-integers).
  - `fromRoman(text)` upper-cases the input so either case works, validates against the strict Roman grammar `^M{0,3}(CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$`, then parses two-letter subtractive pairs before single letters. Invalid input (including `''`) throws `SyntaxError: Invalid Roman numeral: <text>`.
- `src/roman.test.ts`
  - `toRoman` conversion cases including subtractive forms and edge values 1 and 3999.
  - `toRoman` `RangeError` cases: `0`, `-1`, `4000`, `1.5`, `NaN`, `Infinity`.
  - `fromRoman` parsing cases, including lowercase/mixed case (`mcmlxxxiv`, `Ix`).
  - `fromRoman` `SyntaxError` cases: `''`, `IIII`, `VX`, `IC`, `MMMM`, `hello`, `IIV`, `XIIX`.
  - Round-trip `fromRoman(toRoman(n)) === n` for every `n` from 1 to 3999.

Decisions not specified in the plan
- `SyntaxError` message format: `Invalid Roman numeral: <text>`, so the offending text is always named.

Checks
- `bun test`: 57 pass, 0 fail, 4055 expect() calls across 1 file.
- `bunx tsc --noEmit --strict --target es2022 --module esnext --moduleResolution bundler src/roman.ts`: clean (no output).

Committed on `work/main` as two commits:
- `3c9698d` Implement toRoman and fromRoman
- `595e25b` Add tests for roman numeral conversion

